#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE SIXTH STAGE

Now I saw that they went on to the rise that was a little way off, cast up to be a lookout for pilgrims. That was the place from which Christian had the first sight of Faithful his brother. So here they sat down and rested. They also ate and drank here, and made merry, because they had gotten deliverance from so dangerous an enemy. As they sat this way and ate, Christiana asked the guide if he had come to no harm in the battle. Then said Mr. Great-Heart, No, except a little on my flesh; yet even that will be so far from being to my detriment that it is at present a proof of my love to my master and you, and will be a means, by grace, to increase my reward at last.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: But were you not afraid, good sir, when you saw him come with his club?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: It is my duty, said he, to mistrust my own ability, so that I may have reliance on Him who is stronger than all.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: But what did you think when he fetched you down to the ground at the first blow?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Why, I thought, said he, that my Master himself was served so, and yet it was he who conquered at last. 2 Cor 4:10, 11; Rom 8:37.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: When you all have thought what you please, I think God has been wonderfully good to us, both in bringing us out of this valley, and in delivering us out of the hand of this enemy. For my part, I see no reason why we should distrust our God any more, since he has now, and in such a place as this, given us such testimony of his love. Then they got up, and went forward.]

Now a little before them stood an oak; and under it, when they came to it, they found an old pilgrim fast asleep. They knew that he was a pilgrim by his clothes, and his staff, and his girdle.

So the guide, Mr. Great-Heart, woke him; and the old gentleman, as he lifted up his eyes, cried out, What’s the matter? Who are you, and what is your business here?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Come, man, do not be so hot; there are none here but friends. Yet the old man gets up, and stands upon his guard, and will know from them what they are. Then said the guide, My name is Great-Heart: I am the guide of these pilgrims who are going to the Celestial country.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then said Mr. Honest, I beg your pardon: I feared that you were of the company of those who some time ago robbed Little-Faith of his money; but now that I look better about me, I perceive you are honester people.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Why, what would or could you have done to help yourself, if we had indeed been of that company?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Done! Why, I would have fought as long as there was breath in me: and had I done so, I am sure you could never have given me the worst of it; for a Christian can never be overcome, unless he yields of himself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Well said, father Honest, said the guide; for by this I know you are a cock of the right kind, for you have told the truth.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: And by this also I know that you know what true pilgrimage is; for all others think that we are the soonest overcome of any.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Well, now that we are so happily met, please let me ask your name, and the name of the place you came from.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: My name I cannot tell you, but I came from the town of Stupidity: it lies about four degrees beyond the city of Destruction.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Oh, are you from that country? Then I think I have half a guess at you: your name is Old Honesty, is it not?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: So the old gentleman blushed, and said, Not honesty in the abstract, but Honest is my name; and I wish that my nature may agree with what I am called. But, sir, said the old gentleman, how could you guess that I am such a man, since I came from such a place?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I had heard of you before, from my Master; for he knows all things that are done on the earth. But I have often wondered that any should come from your place; for your town is worse than the city of Destruction itself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, we lie farther off from the sun, and so are more cold and numb. But were a man in a mountain of ice, yet if the Sun of righteousness will arise upon him, his frozen heart shall feel a thaw; and so it has been with me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I believe it, father Honest, I believe it; for I know the thing is true.]

Then the old gentleman greeted all the pilgrims with a holy kiss of love, and asked them their names, and how they had fared since they set out on their pilgrimage.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then said Christiana, My name I suppose you have heard of; good Christian was my husband, and these four are his children. But can you imagine how the old gentleman was moved when she told him who she was? He skipped, he smiled, he blessed them with a thousand good wishes, saying,]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: I have heard much of your husband, and of his travels and wars which he underwent in his days. Let it be said to your comfort, the name of your husband rings all over these parts of the world: his faith, his courage, his enduring, and his sincerity under all, had made his name famous. Then he turned to the boys, and asked them their names, which they told him. Then he said to them, Matthew, be like Matthew the tax collector, not in vice, but in virtue. Matt 10:3. Samuel, said he, be like Samuel the prophet, a man of faith and prayer. Ps 99:6. Joseph, said he, be like Joseph in Potiphar’s house, chaste, and one who flees from temptation. Gen 39. And James, be like James the just, and like James the brother of our Lord. Acts 1:13. Then they told him of Mercy, and how she had left her town and her kindred to come along with Christiana and with her sons. At that the old honest man said, Mercy is your name: by mercy you shall be sustained and carried through all those difficulties that shall assault you on your way, till you come there where you shall look the Fountain of mercy in the face with comfort. All this while the guide, Mr. Great-Heart, was very well pleased, and smiled upon his companions.]

Now, as they walked along together, the guide asked the old gentleman if he did not know one Mr. Fearing, who came on pilgrimage out of his parts.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, very well, said he. He was a man who had the root of the matter in him; but he was one of the most troublesome pilgrims that I ever met with in all my days.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I perceive you knew him, for you have given a very right description of him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Knew him! I was a great companion of his; I was with him nearly always; when he first began to think upon what would come upon us hereafter, I was with him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I was his guide from my Master’s house to the gates of the Celestial City.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then you knew him to be a troublesome one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I did so; but I could very well bear it; for men of my calling are often entrusted with the charge of such as he was.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Well then, please let us hear a little of him, and how he managed himself under your guidance.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Why, he was always afraid that he would come short of where he had a desire to go. Everything frightened him that he heard anybody speak of, if it had but the least appearance of opposition in it. I heard that he lay roaring at the Slough of Despond for more than a month together; nor dared he venture, for all that he saw several go over before him, though many of them offered to lend him their hands. He would not go back again, either. The Celestial City—he said he would die if he did not come to it; and yet he was dejected at every difficulty, and stumbled at every straw that anybody cast in his way. Well, after he had lain at the Slough of Despond a great while, as I have told you, one sunshiny morning, I do not know how, he ventured, and so got over; but when he was over, he would hardly believe it. He had, I think, a Slough of Despond in his mind, a slough that he carried everywhere with him, or else he could never have been as he was. So he came up to the gate, you know what I mean, that stands at the head of this way, and there also he stood a good while before he would venture to knock. When the gate was opened, he would draw back, and give place to others, and say that he was not worthy. For, though he got to the gate before some, yet many of them went in before him. There the poor man would stand shaking and shrinking; I dare say it would have moved one’s heart to pity to have seen him. Nor would he go back again. At last he took the hammer that hung on the gate in his hand, and gave a small rap or two; then one opened to him, but he shrank back as before. He who opened stepped out after him, and said, You trembling one, what do you want? With that he fell down to the ground. He who spoke to him wondered to see him so faint, so he said to him, Peace be to you; up, for I have set open the door to you; come in, for you are blessed. With that he got up, and went in trembling; and when he was in, he was ashamed to show his face. Well, after he had been received there a while, as you know the manner is, he was told to go on his way, and also told the way he should take. So he went on till he came out to our house; but as he behaved himself at the gate, so he did at my Master the Interpreter’s door. He lay thereabouts in the cold a good while before he would venture to call; yet he would not go back: and the nights were long and cold then. Indeed, he had a note of necessity in his bosom to my master, to receive him, and grant him the comfort of his house, and also to allow him a stout and valiant conductor, because he was himself so chicken-hearted a man; and yet for all that he was afraid to call at the door. So he hung about up and down thereabouts till, poor man, he was almost perished with cold; yes, so great was his dejection that, though he saw several others get in by knocking, yet he was afraid to venture. At last, I think, I looked out of the window, and perceiving a man to be up and down about the door, I went out to him, and asked what he was: but, poor man, the water stood in his eyes; so I perceived what he wanted. So I went in, and told it in the house, and we made the thing known to our Lord: so he sent me out again, to entreat him to come in; but I dare say I had hard work to do it. At last he came in; and I will say that for my Lord, he dealt wonderfully lovingly with him. There were only a few good bits at the table, but some of it was laid upon his trencher. Then he presented the note; and my Lord looked at it, and said his desire should be granted. So when he had been there a good while, he seemed to get some heart, and to be a little more comforted. For my Master, you must know, is one of a very tender heart, especially to those who are afraid; so he behaved toward him in the way that might tend most to his encouragement. Well, when he had had a sight of the things of the place, and was ready to take his journey to go to the city, my Lord, as he did to Christian before, gave him a bottle of spirits, and some comforting things to eat. So we set forward, and I went before him; but the man was a man of few words, only he would sigh aloud.]

When we had come to where the three fellows were hanged, he said that he feared that that would be his end also. Only he seemed glad when he saw the cross and the sepulcher. There I confess he desired to stay a little to look; and he seemed for a while after to be a little cheery. When he came to the Hill Difficulty, he did not hesitate at that, nor did he much fear the lions: for you must know that his troubles were not about such things as these; his fear was about his acceptance at last.

I got him in at the house Beautiful, I think, before he was willing. Also, when he was in, I made him acquainted with the damsels of the place; but he was ashamed to be much in company. He desired much to be alone; yet he always loved good talk, and often would get behind the screen to hear it. He also loved much to see ancient things, and to be pondering them in his mind. He told me afterward that he loved to be in those two houses from which he came last, that is, at the gate, and that of the Interpreter, but that he dared not be so bold as to ask.

When we went also from the house Beautiful, down the hill, into the Valley of Humiliation, he went down as well as ever I saw a man in my life; for he did not care how lowly he was, so long as he might be happy at last. Indeed, I think there was a kind of sympathy between that Valley and him; for I never saw him better in all his pilgrimage than he was in that Valley.

Here he would lie down, embrace the ground, and kiss the very flowers that grew in this valley. Lam 3:27–29. He would now be up every morning by break of day, pacing and walking to and fro in the valley.

But when he had come to the entrance of the Valley of the Shadow of Death, I thought I should have lost my man: not because he had any inclination to go back; that he always abhorred; but he was ready to die for fear. Oh, the hobgoblins will have me! the hobgoblins will have me! cried he; and I could not beat him out of it. He made such a noise, and such an outcry here, that had they but heard him, it was enough to encourage them to come and fall upon us.

But this I took very great notice of, that this valley was as quiet when we went through it as ever I knew it before or since. I suppose those enemies here had now a special check from our Lord, and a command not to meddle until Mr. Fearing had passed over it.

It would be too tedious to tell you of all; we will therefore only mention a passage or two more. When he had come to Vanity Fair, I thought he would have fought with all the men in the fair. I feared there we should both have been knocked on the head, so hot was he against their follies. Upon the Enchanted Ground he was very wakeful. But when he had come to the river where there was no bridge, there again he was in a heavy state. Now, now, he said, he should be drowned forever, and so never see with comfort that face he had come so many miles to behold.

And here also I took notice of what was very remarkable: the water of that river was lower at this time than ever I saw it in all my life; so he went over at last, not much more than wet-footed. When he was going up to the gate, I began to take leave of him, and to wish him a good reception above. So he said, I shall, I shall. Then we parted, and I saw him no more.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then it seems he was well at last?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, yes, I never had any doubt about him. He was a man of a choice spirit, only he was always kept very low, and that made his life so burdensome to himself, and so troublesome to others. Ps 88. He was, above many, tender about sin: he was so afraid of doing injuries to others that he often would deny himself what was lawful, because he would not cause others to stumble. Rom 14:21; 1 Cor 8:13.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: But what should be the reason that such a good man should be all his days so much in the dark?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: There are two sorts of reasons for it. One is, the wise God will have it so: some must pipe, and some must weep. Matt 11:16. Now Mr. Fearing was one who played upon the bass. He and his fellows sound the sackbut, whose notes are more doleful than the notes of other music are: though indeed, some say, the bass is the ground of music. And for my part, I care not at all for that profession of religion which does not begin in heaviness of mind. The first string that the musician usually touches is the bass, when he intends to put all in tune. God also plays upon this string first, when he sets the soul in tune for himself. Only there was the imperfection of Mr. Fearing; he could play upon no other music but this until toward his latter end.]

\[I make bold to talk thus metaphorically for the ripening of the wits of young readers, and because, in the book of Revelation, the saved are compared to a company of musicians, who play upon their trumpets and harps, and sing their songs before the throne. Rev 5:8; 14:2, 3.\]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: He was a very zealous man, as one may see by the account you have given of him. Difficulties, lions, or Vanity Fair, he feared not at all; it was only sin, death, and hell that were a terror to him, because he had some doubts about his share in that celestial country.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You say right; those were the things that were his troublers; and they, as you have well observed, arose from the weakness of his mind about them, not from weakness of spirit as to the practical part of a pilgrim’s life. I dare believe that, as the proverb is, he could have bitten a firebrand, had it stood in his way; but the things with which he was oppressed, no man ever yet could shake off with ease.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then said Christiana, This account of Mr. Fearing has done me good; I thought nobody had been like me. But I see there was some likeness between this good man and me: only we differed in two things. His troubles were so great that they broke out; but mine I kept within. His also lay so hard upon him that they made him unable to knock at the houses provided for hospitality; but my trouble was always such as made me knock the louder.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: If I might also speak my heart, I must say that something of him has also dwelt in me. For I have always been more afraid of the lake, and the loss of a place in paradise, than I have been of the loss of other things. O, thought I, may I have the happiness to have a dwelling there! It is enough, though I part with all the world to win it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Then said Matthew, Fear was one thing that made me think that I was far from having that within me which accompanies salvation. But if it was so with such a good man as he, why may it not also go well with me?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: No fears no grace, said James. Though there is not always grace where there is the fear of hell, yet, to be sure, there is no grace where there is no fear of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Well said, James; you have hit the mark. For the fear of God is the beginning of wisdom; and to be sure, those who lack the beginning have neither middle nor end. But we will here conclude our discourse of Mr. Fearing, after we have sent after him this farewell.]

#quote[“Well, Master Fearing, thou didst fear \
Thy God, and wast afraid \
Of doing any thing, while here, \
That would have thee betrayed. \
And didst thou fear the lake and pit? \
Would others do so too! \
For, as for them that want thy wit, \
They do themselves undo.”]

Now I saw that they still went on in their talk. For after Mr. Great-Heart had made an end with Mr. Fearing, Mr. Honest began to tell them of another, but his name was Mr. Self-will. He claimed to be a pilgrim, said Mr. Honest; but I am persuaded he never came in at the gate that stands at the head of the way.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Did you ever have any talk with him about it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, more than once or twice; but he would always be like himself, self-willed. He cared neither for man, nor argument, nor yet example; what his mind prompted him to, that he would do, and nothing else could he be made to do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Pray, what principles did he hold? for I suppose you can tell.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: He held that a man might follow the vices as well as the virtues of pilgrims; and that if he did both, he would certainly be saved.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: How? If he had said it is possible for the best to be guilty of the vices, as well as to partake of the virtues of pilgrims, he could not have been much blamed; for indeed we are exempted from no vice absolutely, but on condition that we watch and strive. But this, I perceive, is not the thing; but if I understand you right, your meaning is that he was of the opinion that it was allowable to be so.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Aye, aye, so I mean, and so he believed and practiced.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But what grounds did he have for saying so?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Why, he said he had the Scripture for his warrant.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Please, Mr. Honest, give us a few particulars.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: So I will. He said that to have to do with other men’s wives had been practiced by David, God’s beloved; and therefore he could do it. He said that to have more women than one was a thing that Solomon practiced, and therefore he could do it. He said that Sarah and the godly midwives of Egypt lied, and so did saved Rahab, and therefore he could do it. He said that the disciples went at the bidding of their Master, and took away the owner’s donkey, and therefore he could do so too. He said that Jacob got the inheritance of his father by guile and deceit, and therefore he could do so too.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Very base indeed! And are you sure he was of this opinion?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: I heard him plead for it, bring Scripture for it, bring arguments for it, etc.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: An opinion that is not fit to be allowed anywhere in the world!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: You must understand me rightly: he did not say that any man might do this; but that those who had the virtues of those who did such things might also do the same.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But what is more false than such a conclusion? For this is as much as to say that, because good men in the past have sinned out of infirmity, therefore he had leave to do it out of a presumptuous mind; or that, because a child, by a blast of the wind, or because it stumbled at a stone, fell down and defiled itself in the mire, therefore he might willfully lie down and wallow in it like a boar. Who could have thought that anyone could be so far blinded by the power of lust? But what is written must be true: they “stumble at the word, being disobedient—and to this they were appointed.” 1 Pet 2:8. His supposing that those who give themselves up to the vices of godly men may have their virtues is also a delusion as strong as the other. To eat up the sin of God’s people, Hos 4:8, as a dog licks up filth, is no sign that one is possessed of their virtues. Nor can I believe that one who is of this opinion can at present have faith or love in him. But I know you have made strong objections against him; please, what can he say for himself?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Why, he says that to do this as a matter of opinion seems far more honest than to do it, and yet hold the contrary in opinion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: A very wicked answer. For though to let loose the bridle to lusts, while our opinions are against such things, is bad; yet to sin, and plead that one is allowed to do so, is worse: the one makes onlookers stumble by accident, the other leads them into the snare.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: There are many of this man’s mind who do not have this man’s mouth; and that makes going on pilgrimage so little esteemed as it is.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You have said the truth, and it is to be lamented: but he who fears the King of paradise shall come out of them all.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: There are strange opinions in the world. I know one who said it was time enough to repent when we come to die.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Such are not overwise; if that man had had a week to run twenty miles in his life, he would have been loath to put off his journey to the last hour of that week.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: You say right; and yet most of those who count themselves pilgrims do indeed do this. I am, as you see, an old man, and have been a traveler on this road many a day; and I have taken notice of many things.]

I have seen some who have set out as if they would drive all the world before them, who yet, in a few days, have died as those in the wilderness did, and so never got sight of the promised land. I have seen some who promised nothing at first setting out to be pilgrims, and who one would have thought could not have lived a day, who have yet proved very good pilgrims. I have seen some who have run hastily forward, who again, after a little time, have run just as fast back again. I have seen some who have spoken very well of a pilgrim’s life at first, who after a while have spoken as much against it. I have heard some, when they first set out for paradise, say positively that there is such a place, who, when they have been almost there, have come back again and said there is none. I have heard some boast what they would do in case they should be opposed, who have, even at a false alarm, fled faith, the pilgrim’s way, and all.

Now, as they were thus on their way, there came one running to meet them, and said, Gentlemen, and you of the weaker sort, if you love life, shift for yourselves, for the robbers are before you.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then said Mr. Great-Heart, They are the three who set upon Little-Faith some time ago. Well, said he, we are ready for them: so they went on their way. Now at every turning they looked to meet with the villains; but whether they heard of Mr. Great-Heart, or whether they had some other game, they did not come up to the pilgrims.]

Christiana then wished for an inn to refresh herself and her children, because they were weary. Then said Mr. Honest, There is one a little before us, where a very honorable disciple, one Gaius, lives. Rom 16:23. So they all decided to turn in there; and all the more because the old gentleman gave him so good a report. When they came to the door they went in, not knocking, for folks do not usually knock at the door of an inn. Then they called for the master of the house, and he came to them. So they asked if they might lodge there that night.

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Yes, gentlemen, if you are true men; for my house is for none but pilgrims. Then Christiana, Mercy, and the boys were the more glad, because the innkeeper was a lover of pilgrims. So they called for rooms, and he showed them one for Christiana and her children and Mercy, and another for Mr. Great-Heart and the old gentleman.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then said Mr. Great-Heart, Good Gaius, what do you have for supper? for these pilgrims have come far today, and are weary.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: It is late, said Gaius, so we cannot conveniently go out to seek food; but you shall be welcome to such as we have, if that will satisfy you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: We will be content with what you have in the house; for since I have tested you, I know you are never without what is fitting.]

Then he went down and spoke to the cook, whose name was Taste-that-which-is-good, to get supper ready for so many pilgrims. This done, he comes up again, saying, Come, my good friends, you are welcome to me, and I am glad that I have a house to entertain you in; and while supper is being made ready, if you please, let us entertain one another with some good discourse: so they all said, Agreed.

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Then said Gaius, Whose wife is this aged matron? and whose daughter is this young damsel?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: This woman is the wife of one Christian, a pilgrim of former times; and these are his four children. The maid is one of her acquaintances, one whom she has persuaded to come with her on pilgrimage. The boys take after their father in everything, and long to tread in his steps; indeed, if they but see any place where the old pilgrim has lain, or any print of his foot, it brings joy to their hearts, and they long to lie or tread in the same.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Then said Gaius, Is this Christian’s wife, and are these Christian’s children? I knew your husband’s father, indeed, also his father’s father. Many have been good of this stock; their ancestors lived first at Antioch. Acts 11:26. Christian’s forefathers (I suppose you have heard your husband talk of them) were very worthy men. They have, above any that I know, shown themselves men of great virtue and courage for the Lord of the pilgrims, his ways, and those who loved him. I have heard of many of your husband’s relations who have stood all trials for the sake of the truth. Stephen, who was one of the first of the family from which your husband sprang, was knocked on the head with stones. Acts 7:59, 60. James, another of this family, was slain with the edge of the sword. Acts 12:2. To say nothing of Paul and Peter, men of old of the family from which your husband came, there was Ignatius, who was cast to the lions; Romanus, whose flesh was cut in pieces from his bones; and Polycarp, who played the man in the fire. There was he who was hung up in a basket in the sun for the wasps to eat; and he whom they put into a sack and cast into the sea to be drowned. It would be utterly impossible to count up all of that family who have suffered injuries and death for the love of a pilgrim’s life. Nor can I help being glad to see that your husband has left behind him four such boys as these. I hope they will bear up their father’s name, and tread in their father’s steps, and come to their father’s end.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Indeed, sir, they are likely lads: they seem to choose their father’s ways heartily.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: That is what I said. So Christian’s family is likely still to spread abroad upon the face of the ground, and yet to be numerous upon the face of the earth; let Christiana look out some damsels for her sons, to whom they may be betrothed, etc., so that the name of their father, and the house of his forefathers, may never be forgotten in the world.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: It is a pity his family should fall and be extinct.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Fall it cannot, but be diminished it may; but let Christiana take my advice, and that is the way to uphold it. And, Christiana, said this innkeeper, I am glad to see you and your friend Mercy together here, a lovely pair. And if I may advise, take Mercy into a nearer relation to you: if she will, let her be given to Matthew, your eldest son. It is the way to preserve a posterity in the earth. So this match was concluded, and in process of time they were married: but more of that hereafter.]

Gaius also proceeded, and said, I will now speak on behalf of women, to take away their reproach. For as death and the curse came into the world by a woman, Gen 3, so also did life and health: God sent forth his Son, made of a woman. Gal 4:4. Indeed, to show how much those who came after abhorred the act of the mother, this sex in the Old Testament longed for children, if perhaps this or that woman might be the mother of the Savior of the world. I will say again that when the Savior had come, women rejoiced in him before either man or angel. Luke 1:42–46. I do not read that any man ever gave Christ so much as one groat; but the women followed him, and ministered to him of their substance. Luke 8:2, 3. It was a woman who washed his feet with tears, Luke 7:37–50, and a woman who anointed his body at the burial. John 11:2; 12:3. They were women who wept when he was going to the cross, Luke 23:27, and women who followed him from the cross, Matt 27:55, 56; Luke 23:55, and sat opposite his sepulcher when he was buried. Matt 27:61. They were women who were first with him on his resurrection morning, Luke 24:1, and women who first brought tidings to his disciples that he was risen from the dead. Luke 24:22, 23. Women therefore are highly favored, and show by these things that they are sharers with us in the grace of life.

Now the cook sent up word that supper was almost ready, and sent one to lay the cloth, and the trenchers, and to set the salt and bread in order.

Then said Matthew, The sight of this cloth, and of this forerunner of the supper, begets in me a greater appetite for my food than I had before.

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: So let all the doctrines ministered to you in this life beget in you a greater desire to sit at the supper of the great King in his kingdom; for all preaching, books, and ordinances here are but as the laying of the trenchers, and the setting of salt upon the table, when compared with the feast which our Lord will make for us when we come to his house.]

So supper came up. And first a heave-shoulder and a wave-breast were set on the table before them, to show that they must begin their meal with prayer and praise to God. With the heave-shoulder David lifted up his heart to God; and with the wave-breast, where his heart lay, he used to lean upon his harp when he played. Lev 7:32–34; 10:14, 15; Ps 25:1; Heb 13:15. These two dishes were very fresh and good, and they all ate heartily of them.

The next they brought up was a bottle of wine, as red as blood. Deut 32:14; Judg 9:13; John 15:5. So Gaius said to them, Drink freely; this is the true juice of the vine, that makes glad the heart of God and man. So they drank and were merry.

The next was a dish of milk well crumbed; Gaius said, Let the boys have that, so that they may grow by it. 1 Pet 2:1, 2.

Then they brought up in turn a dish of butter and honey. Then said Gaius, Eat freely of this, for this is good to cheer up and strengthen your judgments and understandings. This was our Lord’s dish when he was a child: “Butter and honey He shall eat, that He may know to refuse the evil and choose the good.” Isa 7:15.

Then they brought them up a dish of apples, and they were very good-tasting fruit. Then said Matthew, May we eat apples, since it was such fruit by and with which the serpent beguiled our first mother?

Then said Gaius,

#quote[“Apples were they with which we were beguil’d, \
Yet sin, not apples, hath our souls defil’d: \
Apples forbid, if ate, corrupt the blood; \
To eat such, when commanded, does us good: \
Drink of his flagons then, thou church, his dove, \
And eat his apples, who art sick of love.”]

Then said Matthew, I raised the scruple because a while ago I was sick from eating fruit.

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Forbidden fruit will make you sick; but not what our Lord has allowed.]

While they were thus talking, they were presented with another dish, and it was a dish of nuts. Song 6:11. Then said some at the table, Nuts spoil tender teeth, especially the teeth of children: which when Gaius heard, he said,

#quote[“Hard texts are nuts, (I will not call them cheaters,) \
Whose shells do keep the kernel from the eaters: \
Open the shells, and you shall have the meat; \
They here are brought for you to crack and eat.”]

Then they were very merry, and sat at the table a long time, talking of many things. Then said the old gentleman, My good landlord, while we are cracking your nuts, if you please, open this riddle:

#quote[“A man there was, though some did count him mad, \
The more he cast away, the more he had.”]

Then they all gave good heed, wondering what good Gaius would say; so he sat still a while, and then replied thus:

#quote[“He who bestows his goods upon the poor, \
Shall have as much again, and ten times more.”]

Then said Joseph, I dare say, sir, I did not think you could have found it out.

Oh, said Gaius, I have been trained up in this way a great while: nothing teaches like experience. I have learned from my Lord to be kind, and have found by experience that I have gained by it. There is one who scatters, and yet increases; and there is one who withholds more than is right, but it tends to poverty: there is one who makes himself rich, yet has nothing; there is one who makes himself poor, yet has great riches. Prov 11:24; 13:7.

Then Samuel whispered to Christiana, his mother, and said, Mother, this is a very good man’s house: let us stay here a good while, and let my brother Matthew be married here to Mercy, before we go any further. Gaius the host, overhearing this, said, With a very good will, my child.

So they stayed there more than a month, and Mercy was given to Matthew as his wife.

While they stayed here, Mercy, as her custom was, would be making coats and garments to give to the poor, by which she brought a very good report upon the pilgrims.

But to return again to our story: After supper the lads desired a bed, for they were weary with traveling: Then Gaius called for someone to show them their room; but Mercy said, I will put them to bed. So she put them to bed, and they slept well: but the rest sat up all night; for Gaius and they were such suitable company that they could not tell how to part. After much talk of their Lord, themselves, and their journey, old Mr. Honest, he who put forth the riddle to Gaius, began to nod. Then said Great-Heart, What, sir, you begin to be drowsy; come, rouse yourself, now here is a riddle for you. Then said Mr. Honest, Let us hear it. Then replied Mr. Great-heart,

#quote[“He that would kill, must first be overcome: \
Who live abroad would, first must die at home.”]

Ha, said Mr. Honest, it is a hard one; hard to expound, and harder to practice. But come, landlord, said he, I will, if you please, leave my part to you: you expound it, and I will hear what you say.

No, said Gaius, it was put to you, and it is expected that you should answer it. Then said the old gentleman,

#quote[“He first by grace must conquered be, \
That sin would mortify; \
Who that he lives would convince me, \
Unto himself must die.”]

It is right, said Gaius; good doctrine and experience teach this. For, first, until grace displays itself, and overcomes the soul with its glory, the soul is altogether without heart to oppose sin. Besides, if sin is Satan’s cords, by which the soul lies bound, how should it make resistance before it is loosed from that infirmity? Secondly, nor will anyone who knows either reason or grace believe that a man who is a slave to his own corruptions can be a living monument of grace. And now that it comes into my mind, I will tell you a story worth the hearing. There were two men who went on pilgrimage; the one began when he was young, the other when he was old. The young man had strong corruptions to grapple with; the old man’s were weak with the decays of nature. The young man kept his steps as even as the old one did, and was in every way as light as he. Who now, or which of them, had their graces shining clearest, since both seemed to be alike?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: The young man’s, doubtless. For that which makes headway against the greatest opposition gives the best proof that it is strongest; especially when it also keeps pace with that which does not meet with half so much, as to be sure old age does not. Besides, I have observed that old men have blessed themselves with this mistake; namely, taking the decays of nature for a gracious conquest over corruptions, and so have been apt to beguile themselves. Indeed, old men who are gracious are best able to give advice to those who are young, because they have seen most of the emptiness of things: but yet, for an old and a young man to set out both together, the young one has the advantage of the clearest showing of a work of grace within him, though the old man’s corruptions are naturally the weakest. Thus they sat talking till break of day.]

Now, when the family were up, Christiana told her son James to read a chapter; so he read the 53rd of Isaiah. When he had done, Mr. Honest asked why it was said that the Savior was to come “out of dry ground,” and also that “He had no form or comeliness.”

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then said Mr. Great-Heart, To the first I answer, because the church of the Jews, of which Christ came, had then lost almost all the sap and spirit of religion. To the second I say, the words are spoken in the person of unbelievers, who, because they lack the eye that can see into our Prince’s heart, therefore judge him by the lowliness of his outside; just like those who, not knowing that precious stones are covered over with a homely crust, when they have found one, because they do not know what they have found, cast it away again, as men do a common stone.]

Well, said Gaius, now you are here, and since, as I know, Mr. Great-Heart is good with his weapons, if you please, after we have refreshed ourselves, we will walk into the fields, to see if we can do any good. About a mile from here there is one Slay-good, a giant, who does much harm on the King’s highway in these parts; and I know where his haunt is. He is master of a number of thieves: it would be well if we could clear these parts of him. So they consented and went: Mr. Great-Heart with his sword, helmet, and shield; and the rest with spears and staves.

When they came to the place where he was, they found him with one Feeble-mind in his hand, whom his servants had brought to him, having taken him in the way. Now the giant was rifling him, with a purpose after that to pick his bones; for he was of the nature of flesh-eaters.

Well, as soon as he saw Mr. Great-Heart and his friends at the mouth of his cave, with their weapons, he demanded what they wanted.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: We want you; for we have come to avenge the wrongs of the many pilgrims whom you have slain, when you have dragged them out of the King’s highway: so come out of your cave. So he armed himself and came out, and to battle they went, and fought for over an hour, and then stood still to get their wind.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Slay-Good]: Then said the giant, Why are you here on my ground?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: To avenge the blood of pilgrims, as I told you before. So they went to it again, and the giant made Mr. Great-Heart give ground; but he came up again, and in the greatness of his mind he let fly with such vigor at the giant’s head and sides that he made him let his weapon fall out of his hand. So he struck him, and slew him, and cut off his head, and brought it away to the inn. He also took Feeble-mind the pilgrim, and brought him with him to his lodgings. When they had come home, they showed his head to the family, and set it up, as they had done others before, for a terror to those who might hereafter attempt to do as he had done.]

Then they asked Mr. Feeble-Mind how he fell into his hands.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Then said the poor man, I am a sickly man, as you see: and because death usually knocked at my door once a day, I thought I should never be well at home; so I took myself to a pilgrim’s life, and have traveled here from the town of Uncertain, where I and my father were born. I am a man of no strength at all of body, nor yet of mind, but would, if I could, though I can only crawl, spend my life in the pilgrim’s way. When I came to the gate that is at the head of the way, the Lord of that place received me freely; he did not object to my weakly looks, nor to my feeble mind; but gave me such things as were necessary for my journey, and told me to hope to the end. When I came to the house of the Interpreter, I received much kindness there: and because the hill of Difficulty was judged too hard for me, I was carried up it by one of his servants. Indeed, I have found much relief from pilgrims, though none were willing to go as slowly as I am forced to do: yet always as they came on, they told me to be of good cheer, and said that it was the will of their Lord that comfort should be given to the feeble-minded, 1 Thess 5:14; and so went on at their own pace. When I had come to Assault-lane, then this giant met me, and told me to prepare for an encounter. But, alas, feeble one that I was, I had more need of a cordial; so he came up and took me. I imagined he would not kill me. Also when he had got me into his den, since I did not go with him willingly, I believed I should come out alive again; for I have heard that no pilgrim who is taken captive by violent hands, if he keeps his heart whole toward his Master, is, by the laws of providence, to die by the hand of the enemy. Robbed I expected to be, and robbed to be sure I am; but I have, as you see, escaped with my life, for which I thank my King as the author, and you as the means. Other blows I also expect; but this I have resolved on, that is, to run when I can, to go when I cannot run, and to creep when I cannot go. As to the main thing, I thank him who loved me, I am settled; my way is before me, my mind is beyond the river that has no bridge, though I am, as you see, but of a feeble mind.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then said old Mr. Honest, Were you not, some time ago, acquainted with one Mr. Fearing, a pilgrim?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Acquainted with him! Yes, he came from the town of Stupidity, which lies four degrees to the north of the city of Destruction, and as many off from where I was born: yet we were well acquainted, for indeed he was my uncle, my father’s brother. He and I have been much of a temper: he was a little shorter than I, but yet we were much alike in make.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: I perceive you knew him, and I am apt to believe also that you were related to one another; for you have his pale look, a cast like his in your eye, and your speech is much alike.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Most who have known us both have said so: and, besides, what I have read in him I have for the most part found in myself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Come, sir, said good Gaius, be of good cheer; you are welcome to me, and to my house. Whatever you have a mind to, call for freely; and whatever you would have my servants do for you, they will do it with a ready mind.]

Then said Mr. Feeble-mind, This is an unexpected favor, and like the sun shining out of a very dark cloud. Did giant Slay-good intend me this favor when he stopped me, and resolved to let me go no further? Did he intend that after he had rifled my pockets I should go to Gaius my host? Yet so it is.

Now, just as Mr. Feeble-mind and Gaius were thus in talk, there came one running, and called at the door, and said that about a mile and a half off there was one Mr. Not-right, a pilgrim, struck dead upon the spot where he was, with a thunderbolt.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Alas! said Mr. Feeble-mind, is he slain? He overtook me some days before I came as far as this, and would keep me company. He was also with me when Slay-good the giant took me, but he was nimble of his heels, and escaped; but it seems he escaped to die, and I was taken to live.]

#quote[“What one would think doth seek to slay outright, \
Ofttimes delivers from the saddest plight. \
That very Providence whose face is death, \
Doth ofttimes to the lowly life bequeath. \
I taken was, he did escape and flee; \
Hands cross’d gave death to him and life to me.”]

Now, about this time Matthew and Mercy were married; also Gaius gave his daughter Phebe to James, Matthew’s brother, as his wife; after which time they stayed about ten days more at Gaius’ house, spending their time and the seasons as pilgrims are used to doing.

When they were to depart, Gaius made them a feast, and they ate and drank, and were merry. Now the hour had come that they must be gone; so Mr. Great-heart called for a reckoning. But Gaius told him that at his house it was not the custom for pilgrims to pay for their lodging. He boarded them by the year, but looked for his pay from the good Samaritan, who had promised him, at his return, faithfully to repay him whatever expense he was put to for them. Luke 10:34, 35. Then said Mr. Great-heart to him,

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Beloved, you are faithful in what you are doing for the brothers, and for strangers, who have testified to the church about your love. You will do well to send them on their way in a manner worthy of God. 3 John 5, 6. Then Gaius took his leave of them all, and of his children, and particularly of Mr. Feeble-mind. He also gave him something to drink by the way.]

Now Mr. Feeble-mind, when they were going out of the door, made as if he intended to linger. When Mr. Great-Heart saw this, he said, Come, Mr. Feeble-mind, please go along with us: I will be your guide, and you shall fare as the rest.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Alas! I lack a suitable companion. You are all hearty and strong, but I, as you see, am weak; I choose, therefore, rather to come behind, lest, by reason of my many infirmities, I should be a burden both to myself and to you. I am, as I said, a man of a weak and feeble mind, and shall be offended and made weak by that which others can bear. I shall like no laughing; I shall like no showy clothes; I shall like no unprofitable questions. Indeed, I am so weak a man as to be offended with that which others have liberty to do. I do not yet know all the truth: I am a very ignorant Christian man. Sometimes, if I hear some rejoice in the Lord, it troubles me because I cannot do so too. It is with me as it is with a weak man among the strong, or as with a sick man among the healthy, or as a lamp despised; so that I do not know what to do. “He who is ready to slip with his feet is like a lamp despised in the thought of him who is at ease.” Job 12:5.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But, brother, said Mr. Great-Heart, I have it in my commission to comfort the feeble-minded, and to support the weak. You must go along with us; we will wait for you; we will lend you our help; we will deny ourselves some things, both of opinion and of practice, for your sake; we will not enter into doubtful disputations before you; we will be made all things to you, rather than you should be left behind. 1 Thess 5:14; Rom 14; 1 Cor 8:9–13; 9:22.]

Now, all this while they were at Gaius’ door; and behold, as they were thus in the heat of their discussion, Mr. Ready-to-halt came by, with his crutches in his hand, and he also was going on pilgrimage.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Then said Mr. Feeble-mind to him, Man, how did you come here? I was just now complaining that I did not have a suitable companion, but you are just what I wished for. Welcome, welcome, good Mr. Ready-to-halt; I hope you and I may be some help to each other.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Ready-to-Halt]: I shall be glad of your company, said the other; and, good Mr. Feeble-mind, rather than we should part, since we have met so happily, I will lend you one of my crutches.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: No, said he, though I thank you for your good will, I am not inclined to halt before I am lame. However, I think when there is occasion, it may help me against a dog.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Ready-to-Halt]: If either I or my crutches can do you a kindness, we are both at your command, good Mr. Feeble-mind.]

Thus, therefore, they went on. Mr. Great-Heart and Mr. Honest went in front, Christiana and her children went next, and Mr. Feeble-mind came behind, and Mr. Ready-to-halt with his crutches. Then said Mr. Honest,

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Please, sir, now that we are on the road, tell us some profitable things about some who have gone on pilgrimage before us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: With a good will. I suppose you have heard how Christian of old met Apollyon in the Valley of Humiliation, and also what hard work he had to go through the Valley of the Shadow of Death. Also I think you cannot help having heard how Faithful was hard put to it by Madam Wanton, by Adam the First, by one Discontent, and by Shame; four villains as deceitful as a man can meet with on the road.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, I have heard of all this; but indeed good Faithful was hardest put to it by Shame: he was an unwearied one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes; for, as the pilgrim well said, he of all men had the wrong name.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: But please, sir, where was it that Christian and Faithful met Talkative? That one was also a notable one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: He was a confident fool; yet many follow his ways.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: He nearly beguiled Faithful.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, but Christian quickly put Faithful in the way of finding him out.]

Thus they went on until they came to the place where Evangelist met Christian and Faithful, and prophesied to them what would befall them at Vanity Fair. Then said their guide, Somewhere about here Christian and Faithful met Evangelist, who prophesied to them what troubles they would meet with at Vanity Fair.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Do you say so? I dare say it was a hard chapter that he read to them then.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: It was so, but he gave them encouragement with it. But why do we talk about them? They were a couple of lion-like men; they had set their faces like a flint. Do you not remember how undaunted they were when they stood before the judge?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Well, Faithful suffered bravely.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: So he did, and brave things came of it as well; for Hopeful, and some others, as the story relates it, were converted by his death.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Well, but please go on; for you are well acquainted with things.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Of all that Christian met with after he had passed through Vanity Fair, one By-ends was the chief.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: By-ends! What was he?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: A very crafty fellow, a downright hypocrite; one who would be religious whichever way the world went, but so cunning that he would be sure never to lose or suffer for it. He had his mode of religion for every fresh occasion, and his wife was as good at it as he. He would turn from opinion to opinion; yes, and argue for doing so, too. But, as far as I could learn, he came to a bad end with his by-ends; nor did I ever hear that any of his children were ever of any esteem with any who truly feared God.]

Now by this time they had come within sight of the town of Vanity, where Vanity Fair is kept. So, when they saw that they were so near the town, they consulted with one another how they should pass through the town; and some said one thing, and some another. At last Mr. Great-Heart said, I have, as you may understand, often been a conductor of pilgrims through this town. Now, I am acquainted with one Mr. Mnason, Acts 21:16, a Cypriot by nation, an old disciple, at whose house we may lodge. If you think it good, we will turn in there.

Agreed, said old Honest; Agreed, said Christiana; Agreed, said Mr. Feeble-mind; and so they all said. Now you must understand it was evening by the time they got to the outskirts of the town; but Mr. Great-Heart knew the way to the old man’s house. So they came there; and he called at the door, and the old man inside knew his voice as soon as ever he heard it; so he opened the door, and they all came in. Then said Mnason, their host, How far have you#super[+] come today? So they said, From the house of Gaius our friend. I assure you, said he, you have gone a good stretch. You may well be weary; sit down. So they sat down.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then said their guide, Come, what cheer, good sirs? I dare say you are welcome to my friend.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: I also, said Mr. Mnason, bid you welcome; and whatever you need, only say so, and we will do what we can to get it for you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Our great need, a while ago, was lodging and good company, and now I hope we have both.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: As for lodging, you see what it is; but as for good company, that will appear in the trial.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Well, said Mr. Great-Heart, will you take the pilgrims up to their lodging?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: I will, said Mr. Mnason. So he took them to their respective places; and also showed them a very fine dining room, where they might be together and have supper until the time should come to go to rest.]

Now, when they were seated in their places, and were a little cheered after their journey, Mr. Honest asked his landlord if there were many good people in the town.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: We have a few; for indeed they are only a few when compared with those on the other side.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: But how can we manage to see some of them? For the sight of good men, to those who are going on pilgrimage, is like the appearing of the moon and stars to those who are sailing upon the seas.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: Then Mr. Mnason stamped with his foot, and his daughter Grace came up. So he said to her, Grace, go and tell my friends, Mr. Contrite, Mr. Holy-man, Mr. Love-saints, Mr. Dare-not-lie, and Mr. Penitent, that I have a friend or two at my house who would like to see them this evening. So Grace went to call them, and they came; and after greetings were made, they sat down together at the table.]

Then said Mr. Mnason their landlord, My neighbors, I have, as you see, a company of strangers come to my house; they are pilgrims: they come from afar, and are going to Mount Zion. But who, said he, do you think this is? pointing his finger at Christiana. It is Christiana, the wife of Christian, the famous pilgrim, who, with Faithful his brother, was so shamefully treated in our town. At that they stood amazed, saying, We little thought to see Christiana when Grace came to call us; so this is a very comforting surprise. They then asked how she was, and if these young men were her husband’s sons. And when she had told them they were, they said, May the King whom you#super[+] love and serve make you like your father, and bring you where he is in peace.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then Mr. Honest (when they had all sat down) asked Mr. Contrite and the rest what state their town was in at present.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Contrite]: You may be sure we are full of hurry in fair-time. It is hard keeping our hearts and spirits in good order when we are in so encumbered a condition. He who lives in such a place as this is, and has to do with such people as we have, needs a reminder to caution him to take heed every moment of the day.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: But how are your neighbors now for quietness?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Contrite]: They are much more moderate now than formerly. You know how Christian and Faithful were treated at our town; but of late, I say, they have been far more moderate. I think the blood of Faithful lies as a load upon them to this day; for since they burned him, they have been ashamed to burn any more. In those days we were afraid to walk the street; but now we can show our heads. Then the name of a professing Christian was odious; now, especially in some parts of our town (for you know our town is large), religion is counted honorable. Then said Mr. Contrite to them, Please, how does it go with you in your pilgrimage? How is the country disposed toward you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: It happens to us as it happens to wayfaring men: sometimes our way is clean, sometimes foul; sometimes uphill, sometimes downhill; we are seldom certain of anything. The wind is not always at our backs, nor is everyone we meet in the way a friend. We have met with some notable hindrances already, and what are still ahead we do not know; but for the most part, we find true what has been said of old, A good man must suffer trouble.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Contrite]: You talk of hindrances; what hindrances have you met with?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: No, ask Mr. Great-Heart, our guide; for he can give the best account of that.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: We have been beset three or four times already. First, Christiana and her children were beset by two ruffians, who they feared would take away their lives. We were beset by Giant Bloody-man, Giant Maul, and Giant Slay-good. Indeed, we rather beset the last than were beset by him. And this is how it was: after we had been some time at the house of Gaius, my host and the host of the whole church, we had a mind one day to take our weapons with us, and go and see if we could come upon any of those who are enemies to pilgrims; for we heard that there was a notable one around there. Now Gaius knew his haunt better than I, because he lived in those parts. So we looked, and looked, until at last we made out the mouth of his cave; then we were glad, and plucked up our spirits. So we approached his den; and look, when we came there, he had dragged this poor man, Mr. Feeble-mind, into his net by sheer force, and was about to bring him to his end. But when he saw us, supposing, as we thought, that he had another prey, he left the poor man in his hole, and came out. So we fell to it very hard, and he laid about him vigorously; but, in conclusion, he was brought down to the ground, and his head cut off, and set up by the wayside as a terror to such as should afterward practice such ungodliness. That I am telling you the truth, here is the man himself to affirm it, who was like a lamb taken out of the mouth of the lion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Then said Mr. Feeble-mind, I found this true, to my cost and to my comfort: to my cost, when he threatened to pick my bones every moment; and to my comfort, when I saw Mr. Great-Heart and his friends, with their weapons, come so near for my deliverance.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Holy-Man]: Then said Mr. Holy-man, There are two things that those who go on pilgrimage need to possess: courage, and an unspotted life. If they do not have courage, they can never hold on their way; and if their lives are loose, they will make the very name of a pilgrim stink.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Love-Saints]: Then said Mr. Love-saints, I hope this caution is not needed among you; but truly there are many who go upon the road who rather declare themselves strangers to pilgrimage than strangers and pilgrims on the earth.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Dare-Not-Lie]: Then said Mr. Dare-not-lie, It is true. They have neither the pilgrim’s clothing nor the pilgrim’s courage; they do not go uprightly, but all awry with their feet; one shoe goes inward, another outward; and their stockings are out behind: here a rag, and there a rent, to the discredit of their Lord.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Penitent]: These things, said Mr. Penitent, they ought to be troubled about; nor are the pilgrims likely to have that honor put upon them and their Pilgrim’s Progress that they desire, until the way is cleared of such spots and blemishes. Thus they sat talking and spending the time until supper was set upon the table, to which they went, and refreshed their weary bodies; then they went to rest.]

Now they stayed in the fair a great while, at the house of Mr. Mnason, who in the course of time gave his daughter Grace to Samuel, Christian’s son, to be his wife, and his daughter Martha to Joseph.

The time, as I said, that they stayed here was long, for it was not now as in former times. So the pilgrims grew acquainted with many of the good people of the town, and did them what service they could. Mercy, as was her habit, labored much for the poor; so their bellies and backs blessed her, and she was an ornament to her profession of faith there. And, to tell the truth about Grace, Phebe, and Martha, they were all of a very good nature, and did much good in their places. They were also all of them very fruitful; so that Christian’s name, as was said before, was likely to live on in the world.

While they lay here, a monster came out of the woods, and killed many of the people of the town. It would also carry away their children, and teach them to suck its whelps. Now, no man in the town dared so much as face this monster; but all fled when they heard the noise of his coming.

The monster was like no single beast on the earth. Its body was like a dragon, and it had seven heads and ten horns. It made great havoc among children, and yet it was governed by a woman. Rev 17:3. This monster proposed conditions to men; and such men as loved their lives more than their souls accepted those conditions. So they came under it.

Now Mr. Great-Heart, together with those who came to visit the pilgrims at Mr. Mnason’s house, entered into a covenant to go and engage this beast, if perhaps they might deliver the people of this town from the paws and mouth of so devouring a serpent.

Then Mr. Great-Heart, Mr. Contrite, Mr. Holy-man, Mr. Dare-not-lie, and Mr. Penitent went out with their weapons to meet him. Now the monster at first was very rampant, and looked upon these enemies with great disdain; but they so belabored him, being sturdy men at arms, that they made him retreat; so they came home to Mr. Mnason’s house again.

The monster, you must know, had his certain seasons to come out in, and to make his attempts upon the children of the people of the town. At these seasons these valiant worthies watched for him, and assaulted him continually; so much so that in the course of time he became not only wounded, but lame. Also he has not made such havoc among the townsmen’s children as he had done before; and it is truly believed by some that this beast will die of his wounds.

This, therefore, made Mr. Great-Heart and his fellows of great fame in this town; so that many of the people who lacked their taste of things still had a reverent esteem and respect for them. On this account, therefore, these pilgrims did not get much hurt here. True, there were some of the baser sort, who could see no more than a mole, nor understand any more than a beast; these had no reverence for these men, and took no notice of their valor and adventures.
